(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-12-11"] = {"11.1":{"s":"Alcohols have an −OH group on a saturated (sp³) carbon. Phenols have an −OH group directly on a benzene ring. Ethers have an oxygen atom joining two carbon groups (R–O–R′). This chapter covers how to name, make and use all three, and how they react."},"11.2":{"s":"By number of −OH groups: monohydric, dihydric (glycol) and trihydric (glycerol). Monohydric alcohols are primary, secondary or tertiary, depending on how many carbons are attached to the carbon carrying the −OH. Ethers are simple (both groups the same) or mixed (different groups).","w":["Primary, secondary or tertiary describes the carbon that carries the −OH, not the number of carbons in the chain."]},"11.3":{"s":"Common names add 'alcohol' after the alkyl group (methyl alcohol). IUPAC names replace the final -e of the alkane with -ol and number the chain to give the −OH carbon the lowest number (propan-2-ol). Phenol keeps its name, with ring positions numbered. Ethers are named as alkoxyalkanes (methoxyethane).","w":["In IUPAC names the −OH group gets the lowest possible number, even ahead of a double bond."]},"11.4":{"s":"Alcohols are made by hydrolysis of alkyl halides, hydration of alkenes, reducing aldehydes and ketones, and Grignard reactions. Phenol is made from chlorobenzene, cumene or diazonium salts. Hydrogen bonding gives them high boiling points; the lower alcohols dissolve in water, but solubility falls as the carbon chain grows. Phenol is more acidic than alcohols because its phenoxide ion is stabilised by resonance. Reactions include esterification, oxidation, dehydration and ring substitution in phenol.","w":["Reactivity of alcohols towards HX is 3° > 2° > 1°.","Phenol is acidic enough to react with NaOH, but not with sodium bicarbonate."]},"11.5":{"s":"Ethers are made by dehydrating alcohols with concentrated sulfuric acid at a controlled temperature. Another way is the Williamson synthesis: an alkoxide reacts with an alkyl halide. They have low boiling points because they cannot hydrogen-bond with each other. They are fairly unreactive, but hot HI splits them into an alcohol and an alkyl iodide.","w":["Williamson synthesis works best with a primary alkyl halide; a tertiary halide gives an alkene instead of an ether.","Heating ethanol with concentrated H₂SO₄ gives ether at the lower temperature and ethene at the higher one."]},"11.6":{"s":"Uses. Methanol is a solvent for paints and varnishes. Ethanol is a solvent and an antifreeze, and is used in medicines and as a fuel. Phenol is used to make plastics (Bakelite), drugs and antiseptics. Diethyl ether is a solvent and was once used as an anaesthetic."}};
